        <body>
            <div xml:id="d711-a1">

                <pb facs="0791" n="711" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg711"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum.</hi></head>
                <p>Lord Lansdowne has learned with much pleasure that Mr. Hay’s views with regard to
                    the scope of reference to arbitration of the question of the Japanese
                    Government’s right to impose house tax coincides with his own, but his Lordship
                    has received a telegram from the British minister at Tokyo from which it appears
                    that the Japanese minister for foreign affairs has frequently informed him that
                    the United States Government have not protested, and are in agreement with the
                    Japanese Government respecting the interpretation of the disputed clause of the
                    treaty.</p>
                <p>Lord Lansdowne trusts therefore that Mr. Hay may see his way to send further
                    instructions immediately to the American representative at Tokyo requesting him
                    to press for the wider scope of reference to arbitration which is being urged by
                    His British and German colleagues.</p>
                <closer><dateline rendition="#float-left"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-04-19">April 19, 1902</date>.</dateline></closer>
            </div>
